<!-- ════════════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS ════════════════ -->
<section class="section" id="conclusions">
<h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>1. LEO is the new center of gravity.</strong> At {leo_share}% of the active population, Low Earth Orbit has become the primary operating domain. This shift from GEO-dominated communications to LEO broadband and Earth observation has profound implications for spectrum allocation, debris mitigation, and space traffic management.
</div>

<div class="callout warning">
  <strong>2. One operator defines the statistical picture.</strong> Starlink's {starlink_count:,} satellites ({sl_pct}%) dominate every aggregate metric. Any analysis of "average" orbital parameters, inclination trends, or launch rates is effectively an analysis of Starlink unless the constellation is explicitly segmented out. Policy frameworks designed around historical satellite populations may not scale to a single-operator mega-constellation world.
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>3. The growth rate is accelerating, not stabilizing.</strong> With {post2020_pct}% of all active satellites launched since 2020 — and {recent_objects:,} ({recent_pct}%) launched in just the last two years — the orbital population is on a steep growth trajectory. If current launch rates continue, the total active satellite count could double within 2–3 years.
</div>

<div class="callout warning">
  <strong>4. Orbital congestion risk is concentrated but real.</strong> The ~530–550 km LEO shell (Starlink's primary operating altitude) is by far the most densely populated region. Combined with OneWeb (~1,200 km), sun-synchronous observation satellites (~500–800 km), and debris from ASAT tests and collisions, this altitude band requires the most urgent attention for collision avoidance and debris mitigation.
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>5. The catalog data is remarkably current.</strong> With element sets refreshed within days (epochs from {epoch_min} to {epoch_max}), NORAD's tracking network is maintaining high-fidelity orbital data despite the rapid population growth. This is a critical enabler for conjunction assessment and space situational awareness.
</div>

<h3>Outlook</h3>
<p>The active satellite catalog tells a story of transformation. In 2015, the orbital environment was dominated by a few hundred GEO communications satellites and a modest LEO population of government science and Earth observation missions. A decade later, commercial LEO mega-constellations have rewritten the demographics. The policy challenge ahead is clear: regulatory frameworks, licensing processes, and international coordination mechanisms designed for a world of hundreds of satellites must now serve a world of tens of thousands — and soon, potentially hundreds of thousands.</p>

<p>Key areas to watch:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Starlink Gen2 and Kuiper deployments</strong> — both will add thousands more LEO satellites in the 2026–2028 timeframe.</li>
  <li><strong>Chinese LEO broadband constellations</strong> (Guowang / National Network) — early deployment phases are beginning.</li>
  <li><strong>Debris mitigation rule compliance</strong> — with large numbers of satellites reaching end-of-life, post-mission disposal reliability is critical.</li>
  <li><strong>Spectrum sharing in LEO</strong> — as multiple operators crowd the same frequency bands, interference coordination becomes more complex.</li>
</ul>
</section>

